This product is a rebuild of the PyCon US mobile app
(https://github.com/PyCon/pycon-us-mobile), which is licensed under the
Apache License, Version 2.0; that app's licence file states Copyright 2015
Drifty Co. The original app is our functional reference; its data semantics,
texts and research notes informed this project. Parts of our code are that
app's logic rewritten in Python and changed: the feed's processing rules
(src/pycon_us/core/rules.py), the slugs (core/slugs.py), the request signing
(core/signing.py) and the API calls (services/endpoints.py).

The app bundles the Roboto font (Regular, Medium and Bold, version 2.138,
https://github.com/googlefonts/roboto), Copyright 2011 Google Inc., licensed
under the Apache License, Version 2.0. Its files and license are in
flutter_packages/roboto_fonts/.

The brand marks in src/assets/icons/brands/ (Python, PyPI, Mastodon, Bluesky,
X, LinkedIn, YouTube and Discord) are the unmodified SVGs of Simple Icons,
version 13.21.0 (https://github.com/simple-icons/simple-icons), a project
released under CC0 1.0 Universal; its disclaimer says that this does not mean
every icon in it is CC0, and its data lists no other licence for these eight.
The marks themselves are trademarks of their owners; the app shows each one
only beside a link to that brand.

The floor plans in src/assets/maps/ are from the PyCon US mobile app's
repository (src/assets/img/floor-plans/), whose licence is the Apache License,
Version 2.0. The concourse, upper, arena and seaside plans are drawings of the
Long Beach Convention Center's spaces prepared by GES, each with GES Global
Experience Specialists' own copyright notice in its title block, which we keep
as printed. All are re-encoded to WebP at a smaller size. On the campus plan,
the PyCon US wordmark and the venue's logo are painted over; the names and
wordmarks of the venue's spaces on it are the venue's. The expo hall plan is
from that repository too (src/assets/img/pycon-us-2026-floorplan-nologo.jpg),
re-encoded the same way, with its sun-and-palms mark painted over; the logos of
the sponsors, the community groups and the Python Software Foundation on it are
trademarks of their owners. The job fair plan is from that repository too
(src/assets/img/floor-plans/job-fair.jpg), cropped to the floor without its
header and its PyCon US logo, and re-encoded the same way; the logos on it are
trademarks of their owners. The room and venue pin positions and the booth
rectangles in src/pycon_us/data/2026/map.toml are that app's too.

The app bundles an unchanged copy of the public PyCon US 2026 feed
(src/pycon_us/data/2026/conference.json, from
https://us.pycon.org/2026/schedule/conference.json) so that it works offline,
and fetches the feed again when it can. Its sessions, names, biographies and
sponsor texts stay with the Python Software Foundation and their authors; this
project's licence does not cover them. The photos and logos the feed links to
are loaded from their published addresses and are not part of this project.

The keynote and panel texts in src/pycon_us/data/2026/content.toml are written
in our own words from the PyCon US site's keynote speakers page
(https://us.pycon.org/2026/about/keynote-speakers/). The photos they name are
that site's, loaded from its address when shown, and are not part of this
project.

